### Calibration Gap (from the KATA calibration `lisp_eval` step)
```json
{{ calibration_gap | tojson }}
```
The `calibration_gap` field ranges 0.0 (calibrated) to 1.0 (maximum gap). It is |mean stated probability − observed hit rate| over the analyst's own resolved forecasts, and `calibration_direction` says which way (overconfident / underconfident). A null gap with direction `undetermined` means fewer than 5 resolved forecasts — report it as unmeasured, not as calibrated. If absent, Lens 3 (Dunning-Kruger) cannot cross-reference the calibration measurement and must flag the gap.

The `intel` bundle above carries per-item pragmatic-semantics tags (`ontological_mode`, `epistemic_mode`, `constraint_force`, `provenance`) on `intel.news_items` and `intel.hypotheses` plus `intel.certainty_drift_risk` (`low | medium | high`), indicating whether the intel mosaic's items were properly classified by certainty level. A high drift risk means downstream steps may have treated a management quote as an ontological fact or a scenario as a forecast. If `intel.certainty_drift_risk` is absent, Lens 2 (Superforecasting) cannot verify that probabilities were applied to the right epistemic category — flag the gap.

